<section xml:id="sec-7-4-Sine_models" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
	<title component="not-workbook">Sine models</title>
	<title component="workbook">Sine models - Practice exercises</title>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-7-4-deep-breath" component="not-workbook">
        <title>Take a deep breath</title>

        <p>
            It's that time of semester. Exams, papers, projects, and all due soon. And it doesn't help that Halima struggles with executive functioning. Breaking all those big tasks into smaller, more manageable pieces? That's a project in itself. Halima sits at her desk and takes nice deep and calming breaths. <q>You got this,</q> she says to herself. <q>Just breathe in and breathe out.</q>
        </p>

        <p>
            Halima feels more centered and sets a timer for 45 minutes to start working on her math project. The professor asked them to pick something from daily life and find an equation that describes it. Last week she had no idea what to pick, but now it comes to her <ndash/> her breath! She wonders: how could I write an equation that shows how I breathe?
        </p>

        <p>
            The models Halima has seen so far like linear or exponential have graphs that just go up or down. They are always increasing or always decreasing. That sort of model won't work because the air in her lungs increases (as she breathes in), then decreases (as she breathes out), then increases (as she breathes in), then decreases (as she breathes out), and so on. Polynomial models have graphs that go up and down a bit, but they don't keep repeating. How can we write an equation that repeats over and over again?
        </p>

        <p>
            Halima thinks of many things in her life that repeat. The seasons of the year, waves in the ocean, her hormonal cycle. So many things.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-7-4-sine-model" component="not-workbook">
        <title>A sine model</title>

        <p>
            Wait! Halima remembers that trig ratios repeat as we go around a circle. That Ferris wheel her friend Taini was riding. That revolving door her classmate Aksel keeps talking about. Those values repeat. Maybe Halima can model her breathing using a trig function.
        </p>

        <p>
            Halima looks up an equation
            <md>
                A = 0.4\sin(48T) + 2.3
            </md>
            where
            <md>
                A = \text{ volume of air in lungs (in liters) }\sim \text{ dep}
            </md>
            <md>
                T = \text{ time since we started measuring lung volume (in seconds) }\sim \text{ indep}
            </md>
            and the sine function is set to take inputs in degrees.
        </p>

        <p>
            She calculates a few values. When <m>T=0</m> the volume of air is
            <md>
                0.4 \times \boxed{\texttt{sin}}(48 \times 0) + 2.3= 2.3 \text{ liters}
            </md>
            When <m>T=1</m> the volume of air is
            <md>
                0.4 \times \boxed{\texttt{sin}}(48 \times 1) + 2.3= 2.5972579302 \approx 2.6 \text{ liters}
            </md>
            When <m>T=2</m> the volume of air is
            <md>
                0.4 \times \boxed{\texttt{sin}}(48 \times 2) + 2.3= 2.6978087581 \approx 2.7 \text{ liters}
            </md>
        </p>

        <p>
            Halima makes a table of values for <m>T=1, 2, \ldots, 10</m> seconds and rounds each answer to one decimal place.
        </p>

        <tabular>
            <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
            <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
            <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
            <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
